In Python, a function that appends to the list you pass changes your list, as the session above shows. Here is the same code in C++.

```cpp
void add_fee(std::vector<double> costs) {
    costs.push_back(1.50);
}

int main() {
    std::vector<double> mine{250.00, 120.00};
    add_fee(mine);
    std::cout << mine.size() << "\n";
}
```

In C++ a variable *is* its object: a box in memory. Adding `&` to the type makes a second name for a box that already exists.

```cpp
std::vector<double> mine{250.00, 120.00};
std::vector<double>& alias = mine;
std::vector<double> copy = mine;
alias.push_back(1.50);
```

A name declared with `&` is a **reference**: another name for an object that already exists. A reference parameter lets a function reach the caller's object.

```cpp
void apply_fee(double& balance, double fee) {
    balance -= fee;
}

double cash = 1000.00;
apply_fee(cash, 2.50);
std::cout << cash << "\n";
```

